WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.055
Blazor applications often depend on
backend APIs for data retrieval and state

2
00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:07.930
changes.

3
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.983
Those APIs may be called from server-side
code,

4
00:00:12.063 --> 00:00:18.221
browser-delivered WebAssembly code, or a
mix of client and server paths.

5
00:00:18.301 --> 00:00:23.267
The API is the authority for protected
data and operations,

6
00:00:23.347 --> 00:00:30.399
so it must authenticate and authorize
requests independently of what the UI decided

7
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:31.174
to show.

8
00:00:31.254 --> 00:00:38.504
A Blazor WebAssembly client should be
treated like a browser single-page application

9
00:00:38.584 --> 00:00:40.968
from a security standpoint.

10
00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:45.319
It can hold user interface logic and call
services,

11
00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:48.081
but it is not a trusted boundary.

12
00:00:48.161 --> 00:00:54.716
API design should assume that requests
arrive from a browser client and must be

13
00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:56.981
evaluated by the backend.

14
00:00:57.061 --> 00:01:04.609
Cookie-authenticated browser APIs and
bearer-token APIs have different implications.

15
00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:11.841
Cookie-based flows may require antiforgery
protection for unsafe browser requests

16
00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:14.404
and careful cookie settings.

17
00:01:14.484 --> 00:01:20.642
Token-based flows require review of token
audience, lifetime, storage,

18
00:01:20.722 --> 00:01:25.986
refresh behavior, and what parts of the
system can access them.

19
00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:31.330
Browser-accessible tokens and state
require careful design.

20
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.687
If a value is available to browser code,

21
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:40.230
the application should not treat it as a
protected server secret.

22
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.985
The design should minimize what is
exposed,

23
00:01:44.065 --> 00:01:49.428
prefer short-lived and scoped credentials
where appropriate,

24
00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:53.680
and keep authoritative decisions in the
backend.

25
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.329
CORS controls browser cross-origin access
behavior.

26
00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:02.977
It is not authentication, and it is not
authorization.

27
00:02:03.057 --> 00:02:09.414
Allowing a browser origin to call an API
does not mean the API should trust the

28
00:02:09.494 --> 00:02:11.977
caller or skip policy checks.

29
00:02:12.057 --> 00:02:16.527
CORS should describe approved browser
integration,

30
00:02:16.607 --> 00:02:21.771
while identity and authorization remain
separate controls.

31
00:02:21.851 --> 00:02:29.002
Backend-for-frontend and server-mediated
API access are design options when teams

32
00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:35.638
want the server to manage sensitive
coordination or reduce browser exposure.

33
00:02:35.718 --> 00:02:40.783
Those patterns can improve control when
applied carefully,

34
00:02:40.863 --> 00:02:47.716
but they still need clear authentication,
authorization, validation, logging,

35
00:02:47.796 --> 00:02:50.180
and operational ownership.
